module contract;

// Monkey intrusive-list insertion — resource-projection vocabulary version 1.
//
// Card CONSTRUCTOR-RESIDUALS. This module declares ONE trusted assumption about
// ONE upstream function. It verifies nothing: the C body is not analyzed by
// this loader, and a declaration is a premise. A function-level `Safe` that
// rests on this module is `Safe` RELATIVE TO this declaration and is never a
// proof of the Monkey implementation.
//
// ## What the pinned body does
//
// Read at Fluent Bit 66910c10a4d7eafa810b84229cf2dcf7b0f26f97,
// `lib/monkey/include/monkey/mk_core/mk_list.h:47-63`:
//
//     struct mk_list { struct mk_list *prev, *next; };
//
//     static inline void __mk_list_add(struct mk_list *_new, struct mk_list *prev,
//                                      struct mk_list *next)
//     {
//         next->prev = _new;  _new->next = next;
//         _new->prev = prev;  prev->next = _new;
//     }
//
//     static inline void mk_list_add(struct mk_list *_new, struct mk_list *head)
//     {
//         __mk_list_add(_new, head->prev, head);
//     }
//
// It has ONE arm. It allocates nothing, frees nothing, reallocates nothing and
// calls nothing but the header-local `__mk_list_add`, which is also read above
// and does the same. Its whole effect is four pointer stores into the two
// list nodes it is handed.
//
// ## Which mode is faithful, and why it is not a new "published" mode
//
// Card ALIAS-AGGREGATE recorded this callee as needing "the unmodeled
// publication relation (O00 B03/R13)" because it links its first argument into
// a list that outlives the call. That premise is **corrected here by
// measurement**, on exactly the ground card HASH-CONTRACTS already established
// for `flb_hash_table_add`'s reference arm: what this projection reads out of a
// declaration is the caller's per-exit acquire/release accounting, and
// `mk_list_add` MOVES NO OBLIGATION AND DELETES NONE. A caller that owed a
// release before the call owes exactly the same release after it.
//
// `resource_stored(entry, head, ...)` is refused on two independent grounds,
// either fatal, and both are the ones HASH-CONTRACTS measured:
//
//   1. It is FALSE of the body. §C04.2 reads the stored relation as custody
//      indexed beneath the designated owner, whose destruction cascades to the
//      child. Destroying a `struct mk_list` head destroys no member of the list
//      — `mk_list` has no destructor at all — so declaring it stored would
//      DELETE the caller's own cleanup obligation for a resource nothing else
//      releases, which is the direction hard invariant 1 forbids.
//   2. It is UNREACHABLE. §C04.2 requires the `owner` parameter to be declared
//      `Owned`, and at the measured call site the head is reached as
//      `&in->chunks` — a member address of a borrowed instance — so no caller
//      supplies live custody of it.
//
// The faithful mode is therefore `resource_borrowed` on both parameters. Note
// that this is a statement about custody, not about mutation: the call DOES
// write through both arguments, and `resource_borrowed` never claimed
// otherwise — it claims the call requires no resource custody and moves none.
//
// ## What this declaration does NOT say (named, not hidden)
//
// After the call, the list rooted at `head` REACHES the object `entry` is
// embedded in, for as long as that list lives. This projection models custody,
// not reachability: it does not claim the linked node stays valid, does not
// model the list's lifetime, and does not decide whether a later release of the
// enclosing object leaves the list holding a dangling node. That publication is
// O00 blockers B03/R13 and stays unmodeled — the same sentence, for the same
// reason, that `fluent_bit/hash_table_publication.qcore` carries. Declaring
// `resource_borrowed` is a statement about obligations, never a statement that
// the callee forgets the pointer.
//
// Nothing is claimed about `__mk_list_add`, `mk_list_del`, `mk_list_append`,
// `mk_list_add_before`/`_after`, iteration, or the `container_of` idiom by
// which callers recover an enclosing object from a node. `mk_list_del` in
// particular is DELIBERATELY LEFT UNDECLARED: it is the operation that would
// have to say what unlinking does to an obligation, and no call site measured
// by this card needs it. Declaring it would be a premise with no measured
// payoff, which is card PROD-CONTRACTS' rule for `flb_log_is_truncated`.
//
// ## Measured reach, recorded rather than assumed
//
// At `flb_input_chunk_create` this declaration is admitted — the actual
// `&ic->_head` is a member address of a live body-acquired aggregate at a
// declared `Borrowed` position (card O04-B09B10) — and on its own it changes
// the constructor's diagnostic count by ZERO, because the statement before it
// is refused for an unrelated reason and poisons the reaching state. Its
// contribution is measured by withdrawal instead: with the three statements
// this card could not admit removed from the preprocessed text, the
// constructor reports 0 diagnostics and discharges all six exits WITH this
// declaration and 3 diagnostics WITHOUT it.
//
// Corpus-wide the declaration is admitted at THIRTEEN call sites in twelve
// units. Two of them — `chunk_state_sync`'s in `lib/chunkio/src/cio_chunk.c`,
// whose base is a formal the body never binds — are modeled outright and their
// diagnostics disappear. At the other eleven the statement's own destination is
// now admitted and the *aggregate's* unresolved identity is what refuses
// instead, so a refusal replaces a refusal in the fail-closed direction. No
// unit and no function changed verdict. See
// `docs/reports/remaining-constructor-residuals/README.md` §4 and §6.3.

extern fn mk_list_add(entry: ptr<mut, u8>, head: ptr<mut, u8>) -> unit
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(entry);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(head);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};
